', 'Per workplan-co0007-v3 §B1 (operative plan), B1 evaluates ≥3 storage forms against requirements before committing. The framework operationalises that mandate. Four candidates instead of three because the floor is ≥3 and the four named candidates each map to a distinct repo-strategy revisit trigger in governance/repo-strategy.md (no migration; same repo plus DB; sibling repo for graph; per-component decision). 30 criteria because Session-1 sourcing against doctrinal anchors (mission §Doctrinal commitments 1, 3, 6; A6 §§1-4; A5 §6.1; A7; A9; A10; A12; A13) produced 36 distinct requirements across 4 bands; clustering reduced to 30 criteria across 10 evaluable surfaces. Per-criterion 4-question template keeps cross-candidate comparison legible (the alternative — bespoke evaluation per candidate — invites the prejudgment failure mode the framework is designed to prevent). Weighting is deferred to Session 2 deliberately — weighting after candidates are sketched introduces post-hoc bias toward whichever candidate scored well on lightly-considered criteria. The first-pass candidate × cluster sketch in §7 of the framework document is calibration only and is explicitly barred from citation in the eventual selection rationale (§7.2). The framework binds the rest of B1: Sessions 3-6 cannot adopt new criteria without amending this decision.
', '2026-05-01 02:35', 'opus_4_7_session_2026-05-01', 'opus/150/synth', 150, 'ACTIVE', 'PENDING', '[]', '[]', '["workplan/b1-derivation-framework.md", "sessions/session_2026-05-01-b1-s1.md", "data/decisions/decision_register.yaml"]', '["Adopt v3''s named 3-candidate set verbatim (graph DB / RDF triplestore / hybrid) \u2014 rejected: omits Markdown+YAML status-quo-extended which is the lowest-migration-cost candidate and the doctrinal default any storage-form selection must beat to justify its switching cost. The omission would have been a silent prejudgment.", "Single-pass evaluation in one session \u2014 rejected: 4 candidates \u00d7 30 criteria \u00d7 4 questions = 480 evaluations. One session cannot do justice to that volume; compression invites superficial reads of candidates that warrant scrutiny.", "No criteria-weighting at all (treat all criteria as equal) \u2014 rejected: doctrinal-anchor criteria (sourced from mission/A6 commitments) are not equivalent in importance to operational-convenience criteria. Equal weighting silently flattens the doctrinal anchor. Session 2 commits the weighting scheme explicitly.", "Skip the first-pass calibration sketch (\u00a77) \u2014 rejected: without a first-pass surface of where each candidate strains, Sessions 3-6 budget effort uniformly; that wastes sessions on candidate-criterion cells where the answer is already visible. The sketch is bounded by \u00a77.2 to prevent it from becoming prejudgment."]', 'First Decision record under Stage B. The framework binds Sessions 2-9; amending it during Sessions 3-6 requires a superseding D-METH record (e.g., addition of a fifth candidate per §8.2 expansion trigger, or revision of the criterion clusters). The framework explicitly defers (a) weighting scheme to Session 2, (b) selection to Sessions 7-9. This decision is review-pending because the framework is the spine of B1; later sessions inherit its commitments.
', '2026-08-04 19:55', 'session_2026-08-04-f3a-decision-register-import', '2026-08-04 19:55', 'session_2026-08-04-f3a-decision-register-import');
', 'Routine sequencing under the framework established by D-0108. The arc follows the standard Stage A pattern (governance + code per CO-0008) adapted for B1''s derive-then-implement structure (derive in workplan/, implement in architecture/). DG-AUTO because once D-0108 is reviewed and confirmed, the session arc is mechanical application — the compression and expansion triggers are explicit rules, not discretionary judgments. Each session''s primary output is named to keep the arc auditable; deviation from named outputs requires a superseding D-OP record.
', '2026-05-01 02:35', 'opus_4_7_session_2026-05-01', 'opus/100/route', 100, 'ACTIVE', 'NA', '[]', '["D-0108"]', '["workplan/b1-derivation-framework.md \u00a78", "sessions/session_2026-05-01-b1-s1.md"]', '[]', 'D-OP/DG-AUTO does not require alternatives_considered per schema. The arc is mechanical under D-0108; if D-0108 is revised in review, this decision is reviewed as a downstream consequence. Compression and expansion triggers are explicit (§8.1, §8.2 of the framework document) so subsequent sessions can apply them without further capture.
', '2026-08-04 19:55', 'session_2026-08-04-f3a-decision-register-import', '2026-08-04 19:55', 'session_2026-08-04-f3a-decision-register-import');
', 'The framework''s §6.3 specifies that weighting must precede candidate evaluation — weighting after candidates are sketched introduces post-hoc bias toward whichever candidate scored well on lightly-considered criteria. Tiered (3/2/1) was chosen over all-equal because all-equal silently flattens the doctrinal anchor: mission §Doctrinal commitments 1, 3, 6 are foundational claims; T-03/T-04/A6/A9/A10/A12/A13 operationalize them; CI tractability is project survival but not doctrinal. Treating all 30 criteria as equal would silently de-prioritize the mission''s foundational claims relative to operational convenience — the audit-B-01 silent-compression failure mode. Tiered (3/2/1) was chosen over pure doctrinal-anchor-only because pure doctrinal-anchor would assign higher weight only to criteria sourced from mission §Doctrinal commitments and 1 to everything else, which is too coarse — operationalization-binding criteria (e.g., A6 §1.2''s tier+evidence_type orthogonal encoding) are not optional; they are locked governance. The tiered scheme makes the distinction visible: foundational doctrinal (3) > operationalization-binding (2) > operational-preference (1). The assignment rules are mechanical — anyone can audit a weight by reading the criterion''s source citation in §4 of the framework and applying the rule in §3.1 of the weighting document. Rules do not invite per-criterion judgment, which forecloses the failure mode of bespoke weight-creep.
', '2026-05-01 03:50', 'opus_4_7_session_2026-05-01', 'opus/150/synth', 150, 'ACTIVE', 'PENDING', '[]', '["D-0108"]', '["workplan/b1-criteria-weighting.md", "sessions/session_2026-05-01-b1-s2.md", "data/decisions/decision_register.yaml"]', '["All-equal weighting (each criterion 1/30) \u2014 rejected: silently flattens the distinction between foundational doctrinal claims and operational convenience criteria. Would, for example, give mission \u00a73 Co-1 co-primary and \"CI validation tractability\" the same weight in storage selection. The audit-B-01 silent-compression failure mode applies to weights as much as to wording.", "Pure doctrinal-anchor-only (mission-sourced weight 3; everything else weight 1) \u2014 rejected: too coarse. Operationalization-binding criteria sourced to T-03, T-04, A6, A9 are LOCKED governance, not preferences. They are not optional in the way that audit-trail preservation across migration is optional. Treating them at weight 1 would misrepresent their force.", "Per-criterion bespoke weights (every criterion gets a discrete numeric weight per judgment) \u2014 rejected: invites weight-creep over Sessions 3\u20136 as evaluations surface new considerations. The mechanical assignment rule prevents this \u2014 weights are functions of source citations, not of candidate-evaluation findings.", "Weighting deferred to Session 7 (after candidate evaluation) \u2014 rejected: explicitly forbidden by the framework''s \u00a76.3. Weighting after candidates are sketched introduces post-hoc bias."]', 'Sub-clause: C-8.4 (stale-evidence detection at query time) is downgraded from weight 2 (the §3.1 rule''s default for an A9-sourced criterion) to weight 1, because the storage-layer prerequisite is light enough that no candidate''s satisfaction differentiates from another''s — the differentiation happens at the query layer (B2), not the storage layer (B1). The downgrade is captured in §3.2 of the weighting document and reflected in the §4.3 weight-1 listing.
', '2026-08-04 19:55', 'session_2026-08-04-f3a-decision-register-import', '2026-08-04 19:55', 'session_2026-08-04-f3a-decision-register-import');
', 'S-05 from b1-derivation-framework.md §4.4 specified "Migration-out cost is bounded and documented: if B1''s choice is later overturned, the corpus can leave with its semantics intact." That wording is correct as a principle but not evaluable as written. This decision operationalizes the principle into five deliverables that each candidate must demonstrate in Sessions 3–6. Binary pass/fail prevents the failure mode where a candidate scores 4/5 on partial credit and is treated as comparable to a candidate scoring 5/5 — the missing deliverable could be the one that traps the project (e.g., a candidate that exports records but loses cell-level state machine values when overturned silently traps the synthesis work). The 2-session budget for export tooling is anchored to the observed scale of Stage A converters; if a candidate''s export tooling cannot fit in that budget, the migration-out cost is by definition unbounded relative to the project''s normal session economics.
', '2026-05-01 03:50', 'opus_4_7_session_2026-05-01', 'opus/150/synth', 150, 'ACTIVE', 'PENDING', '[]', '["D-0108"]', '["workplan/b1-criteria-weighting.md \u00a73.5", "sessions/session_2026-05-01-b1-s2.md", "data/decisions/decision_register.yaml"]', '["Weighted partial scoring (candidate scores 0\u20135 on the deliverables, with weighted sum) \u2014 rejected: invites the trap where a 4/5 candidate looks comparable to 5/5 but the missing deliverable is the binding-constraint one. Binary pass/fail forces the candidate to satisfy all five.", "Less stringent 3-deliverable standard (full export + relationship preservation + schema mapping) \u2014 rejected: state preservation is not optional. A candidate that exports records but loses verification statuses or state-machine values traps the synthesis work; the project''s epistemic commitments live in those states.", "More stringent (lossless re-import required for all three other candidates) \u2014 rejected: lossless re-import across substrate boundaries (e.g., relational \u2192 graph \u2192 markdown) is not generally achievable; requiring it would dismiss all four candidates by construction. Documented losses are sufficient.", "No 2-session budget cap on export tooling (any tooling cost acceptable as long as documented) \u2014 rejected: a candidate whose export tooling would take 8 sessions to write has effectively unbounded migration-out cost relative to the project''s session economics. The 2-session bound matches the scale of Stage A converters, which is the empirical reference for \"tractable converter complexity.\""]', 'Operationalizes criterion C-10.3 (migration-out cost bounded). C-10.3 holds weight 2 under D-0110 because S-05 is binding under S-05 itself (the requirement is doctrinally privileged insofar as the project''s resolution principle of "clean data + transparent methodology" requires that the corpus not be trapped in a chosen storage form). Sessions 3–6 evaluate each candidate against the five deliverables; the schema mapping document of §4 is itself a deliverable artifact each candidate produces during its evaluation session.
', '2026-08-04 19:55', 'session_2026-08-04-f3a-decision-register-import', '2026-08-04 19:55', 'session_2026-08-04-f3a-decision-register-import');
', 'The framework''s §7.1 conjectured that Candidate D''s hybrid burden may make it strictly dominated by either A-with-query-layer or B-with-prose-as-files. This decision specifies the test that establishes dominance — or fails to. Strict dominance (no criterion where D outscores) was chosen over weighted-sum dominance because a weighted-sum test could eliminate D when D loses on weight-1 criteria but uniquely wins on a weight-3 criterion. That is the wrong dismissal — it would dismiss a candidate that uniquely satisfies a doctrinal-binding criterion. The strict-dominance test ensures D is dismissed only when no single criterion shows it as the best satisfier. The threshold uses strict inequality (D_i > M_i) — a tie does not count as material betterment because the simpler form satisfies equivalently and Candidate D''s hybrid complexity is not justified by tie-equivalent satisfaction.
', '2026-05-01 03:50', 'opus_4_7_session_2026-05-01', 'opus/150/synth', 150, 'ACTIVE', 'PENDING', '[]', '["D-0108"]', '["workplan/b1-criteria-weighting.md \u00a73.6", "sessions/session_2026-05-01-b1-s2.md", "data/decisions/decision_register.yaml"]', '["Weighted-sum dominance (D dismissed if sum(weight_i \u00d7 D_i) < max sum across A, B, C) \u2014 rejected: a candidate uniquely satisfying a weight-3 criterion (e.g., questions-led query mode) but losing on many weight-1 criteria could be wrongly dismissed despite its unique value on the doctrinal-binding criterion.", "Threshold-based dominance (D dismissed if D''s average score < 0.8 \u00d7 average max-of-ABC) \u2014 rejected: same failure mode as weighted-sum, with an arbitrary 0.8 threshold to boot.", "Tie counts as material betterment (D survives if D_i \u2265 M_i for some i) \u2014 rejected: a tie means the simpler form satisfies equivalently. Candidate D''s hybrid burden is not justified by tie-equivalent satisfaction.", "Defer the elimination test to Session 7 (evaluate all four candidates, then decide on methodology) \u2014 rejected: post-hoc elimination methodology design is exactly the failure mode the framework''s \u00a76.3 forbids. The test is specified before evaluation runs."]', 'The eliminator test is specific to Candidate D (Hybrid). Candidates A, B, C are not subject to this test in Sessions 3–5 — they all proceed to Session 7 comparative scoring regardless of their per-criterion scores. The asymmetry is deliberate — the framework §5.4 noted that Candidate D was included because v3''s named candidate set includes hybrid-as-option, but its complexity (two stores'' worth of operational burden against one author) is the explicit reason the framework called it out for an eliminator test.
', '2026-08-04 19:55', 'session_2026-08-04-f3a-decision-register-import', '2026-08-04 19:55', 'session_2026-08-04-f3a-decision-register-import');
', 'Routine confirmation under the framework established by D-0108. Session 2 reviewed §4 of the framework against the governance set already in context (mission, audience-priority, A3, A5, A6, A7, A8, A9, A10, A12, A13, project-standards) and identified no additions or removals warranted by the read. The lock is the procedural commitment that Sessions 3–6 operate against this set; the principle is that frozen requirements force evaluation to confront the actual set rather than a moving target.
', '2026-05-01 03:50', 'opus_4_7_session_2026-05-01', 'opus/100/route', 100, 'ACTIVE', 'NA', '[]', '["D-0108"]', '["workplan/b1-criteria-weighting.md \u00a72", "sessions/session_2026-05-01-b1-s2.md"]', '[]', 'DG-AUTO because the lock is mechanical confirmation under D-0108. The lock principle is the substantive commitment; the act of confirming a particular review found no additions is routine.
', '2026-08-04 19:55', 'session_2026-08-04-f3a-decision-register-import', '2026-08-04 19:55', 'session_2026-08-04-f3a-decision-register-import');
', 'Mechanical application of D-0108 (framework), D-0110 (weighting), D-0111 (S-05 spec), D-0112 (eliminator test — does not apply to Candidate A), D-0113 (requirements lock). Each criterion was assessed against the four-question template from b1-derivation-framework.md §6.1 (native/adapted/absent; adaptation cost; operational risk; downstream binding). Scores follow the D-0112 rubric (0=absent, 1=high-cost adaptation, 2=moderate-cost adaptation, 3=native). The evaluation does not compare A to B/C/D — that is Session 7; it produces A''s standalone score for input to comparative scoring.
The score is not the selection. Sessions 4-6 produce comparable scores for B, C, D; Session 7 compares; Sessions 8-9 select. Candidate A''s 77% baseline is strong but the selection question Sessions 7-8 must answer is whether B, C, or D outscore A enough to justify their migration cost and operational complexity given the project''s pre-launch solo-author posture (D-03 binding).
', '2026-05-02 02:00', 'opus_4_7_session_2026-05-02', 'opus/150/synth', 150, 'ACTIVE', 'NA', '[]', '["D-0108", "D-0110", "D-0113"]', '["workplan/b1-candidate-a-markdown-yaml.md", "sessions/session_2026-05-02-b1-s3.md", "data/decisions/decision_register.yaml"]', '[]', 'DG-AUTO because the evaluation applies the framework + weighting + lock as specified in earlier D-METH records. No methodological commitment is added by this decision; the evaluation is the methodology being applied. Sessions 4 (Candidate B), 5 (Candidate C), 6 (Candidate D + eliminator test) follow the same pattern with their own D-OP/DG-AUTO records.
', '2026-08-04 19:55', 'session_2026-08-04-f3a-decision-register-import', '2026-08-04 19:55', 'session_2026-08-04-f3a-decision-register-import');
', 'The framework §5.2 named three relational sub-substrates (SQLite, PostgreSQL, DuckDB) and deferred the within-Candidate-B sub-decision to Session 4 if Candidate B survives Session 3''s calibration sketch. Session 4 must commit to one sub-substrate to evaluate against the 30 criteria — evaluating against the union of sub-substrates would conflate candidates. SQLite is selected because (a) format longevity is the strongest of the three; (b) solo-author operability matches D-03''s pre-launch binding (no server process, no infrastructure ops); (c) the project''s volume (1,098 records, expected to grow into Stage C but bounded) is well within SQLite''s operational sweet spot — single-file databases up to terabytes are routine for SQLite; the project corpus is megabytes; (d) CI integration cost is lowest (regenerate .db on each push from canonical SQL fixtures); (e) tool independence is high (any SQLite library in any language reads the format). PostgreSQL would be selected if the project required multi-user concurrent writes (it does not — solo-author), or schema-level features SQLite lacks (it does not — A6 state machines, constraints, triggers all available in SQLite). DuckDB would be selected if analytical query performance dominated other concerns (it does not — the audience-priority modes are well-served by indexed SQLite queries given the corpus size).
', '2026-05-02 02:30', 'opus_4_7_session_2026-05-02', 'opus/150/synth', 150, 'ACTIVE', 'PENDING', '[]', '["D-0108"]', '["workplan/b1-candidate-b-relational.md \u00a71.2", "sessions/session_2026-05-02-b1-s4.md", "data/decisions/decision_register.yaml"]', '["PostgreSQL \u2014 rejected: server process adds operational discipline that solo-author pre-launch posture (D-03) does not need. PostgreSQL''s ecosystem advantages (replication, extensions, advanced features) are post-launch concerns at best; pre-launch they are operational overhead without value-add.", "DuckDB \u2014 rejected: file format longevity is materially weaker than SQLite (younger project, single steward); analytical-query strength does not compensate at the project''s data volume; the audience-priority modes are well-served by indexed SQLite.", "Defer the substrate decision to Session 7 (evaluate Candidate B at the abstract \"relational\" level then pick a substrate after) \u2014 rejected: the within-substrate differences are large enough that \"relational\" without a specified substrate is underspecified for evaluation. Sessions 7-9 can revisit if the candidate is selected."]', 'Sub-decision under D-0108. If Candidate B is selected at Sessions 7-9 and a different sub-substrate''s properties later dominate (e.g., Stage C scope reveals a need for analytical queries at scale), this decision can be superseded. The selection is for B1 evaluation purposes; downstream stages may revisit.
', '2026-08-04 19:55', 'session_2026-08-04-f3a-decision-register-import', '2026-08-04 19:55', 'session_2026-08-04-f3a-decision-register-import');
', 'Mechanical application of D-0108 (framework), D-0110 (weighting), D-0111 (S-05 spec), D-0112 (eliminator test — does not apply to Candidate B), D-0113 (requirements lock), D-0129 (within-B substrate = SQLite). Each criterion was assessed against the four-question template from b1-derivation-framework.md §6.1. Scores follow the D-0112 rubric (0=absent, 1=high-cost adaptation, 2=moderate-cost adaptation, 3=native). The evaluation does not compare B to A/C/D — that is Session 7; it produces B''s standalone score for input to comparative scoring.
The score is not the selection. Session 7 compares; Sessions 8-9 select. Candidate B''s 89% is materially higher than Candidate A''s 77%, but the comparison is decisive only against weighted-difference thresholds Session 7 must specify. The 12-point gap reflects Cluster VII (+15 points), III (+3), and II/IV (+5 combined) gains for B over A, partially offset by Cluster IX (-11 points) loss for B vs A.
', '2026-05-02 02:30', 'opus_4_7_session_2026-05-02', 'opus/150/synth', 150, 'ACTIVE', 'NA', '[]', '["D-0108", "D-0110", "D-0113", "D-0129"]', '["workplan/b1-candidate-b-relational.md", "sessions/session_2026-05-02-b1-s4.md", "data/decisions/decision_register.yaml"]', '[]', 'DG-AUTO because the evaluation applies the framework + weighting + lock + substrate choice as specified in earlier D-METH records.
', '2026-08-04 19:55', 'session_2026-08-04-f3a-decision-register-import', '2026-08-04 19:55', 'session_2026-08-04-f3a-decision-register-import');
', 'The framework §5.3 named two graph sub-substrate families (RDF triplestore; property graph) and deferred the within-Candidate-C sub-decision to Session 5 if Candidate C survives Session 1''s calibration sketch. Session 5 must commit to one sub-substrate to evaluate against the 30 criteria — evaluating against the union of sub-substrates would conflate candidates. RDF/Jena is selected because format longevity dominates other concerns at the 12+ year horizon (S-01 binding): W3C-standardized RDF outlives any specific engine; data is portable across Jena/RDF4J/Virtuoso/Stardog/Blazegraph regardless of which engine processes it. Apache Foundation stewardship eliminates the single-vendor risk that disqualifies Blazegraph (deprecated 2020) and constrains Neo4j (single-steward Neo4j Inc.). TDB embedded mode addresses the solo-author posture concern (D-03 binding) by avoiding a server process — Jena runs as a JVM library against a TDB directory, operationally closer to SQLite than to Neo4j or Fuseki server mode.
', '2026-05-02 02:55', 'opus_4_7_session_2026-05-02', 'opus/150/synth', 150, 'ACTIVE', 'PENDING', '[]', '["D-0108"]', '["workplan/b1-candidate-c-graph.md \u00a71.3", "sessions/session_2026-05-02-b1-s5.md", "data/decisions/decision_register.yaml"]', '["Apache Jena Fuseki (server mode) \u2014 rejected: server process adds operational discipline that solo-author pre-launch posture (D-03) does not need. Embedded TDB achieves the same query power without the server.", "Blazegraph \u2014 rejected: project deprecated 2020 (Amazon acquired/forked into Neptune); no active maintenance on the open-source codebase. RDF data would survive (W3C-standardized format), but operating against an unmaintained engine creates compounding risk.", "Neo4j Community Edition \u2014 rejected: single-vendor steward (Neo4j Inc.); Cypher''s openCypher standardization is partial; on-disk format proprietary. Format longevity materially weaker than RDF. Operationally simpler than RDF in some ways but worse on the doctrinal-binding concerns (longevity, vendor independence).", "Memgraph or TigerGraph \u2014 rejected: vendor risk too high for a 12+ year horizon. Both are newer projects with smaller ecosystems and vendor-dependent toolchains."]', 'Sub-decision under D-0108. If Candidate C is selected at Sessions 7-9 and a different sub-substrate''s properties later dominate (e.g., Stage C scope reveals a need for property-graph traversal patterns that property-graph engines optimize better than RDF), this decision can be superseded. The selection is for B1 evaluation purposes; downstream stages may revisit.
