', 'The pivotal criterion was C-5.2 (evidence markers ●/○ enforced per specification sentence) — the only criterion where max(A, B, C) = 2 rather than 3 and thus the only criterion where D could exceed by scoring 3. D scored 2 on C-5.2 because the hybrid form does not provide a structural advantage on sentence-level marker enforcement: spec text is markdown either way, and sentence-level enforcement is determined by how prose is processed and what marker convention is mandated, not by which substrate non-prose data lives in. The structured-marker convention (H1'' variant) that could plausibly achieve score 3 on C-5.2 is form-agnostic — it works in plain markdown (Candidate A) as well as in any hybrid containing markdown. Therefore the structured-marker advantage cannot uniquely belong to the hybrid form.
Three structural observations about hybrid forms (framework §7.1) are confirmed mechanically by this evaluation: (1) a hybrid is bounded above by the best of its constituent substrates on most criteria; (2) operational criteria compound the hybrid burden — D''s Cluster IX scored 33%, at the candidate-set floor; (3) solo-author posture (D-03 binding) is hostile to hybrids because cognitive overhead of two stores is itself a binding constraint. The eliminator test is functioning as designed: D is not dismissed because its weighted total is low (it''s 84%, between A''s 77% and B''s 89%); D is dismissed because no single criterion uniquely favors the hybrid form over the best singleton.
', '2026-05-02 02:25', 'opus_4_7_session_2026-05-02', 'opus/150/synth', 150, 'ACTIVE', 'PENDING', '[]', '["D-0108", "D-0110", "D-0112", "D-0113", "D-0119", "D-0121", "D-0132", "D-0133"]', '["workplan/b1-candidate-d-hybrid.md \u00a74", "sessions/session_2026-05-02-b1-s6.md", "data/decisions/decision_register.yaml"]', '["Survive D on tie-equivalence (D survives if D_i >= M_i for some i) \u2014 rejected by D-0112 itself: a tie means the simpler form satisfies equivalently and the hybrid''s coordination burden is not justified by tie-equivalent satisfaction. The strict-inequality threshold was chosen specifically to dismiss D under exactly these conditions.", "Re-evaluate H1 with structured-marker convention to push C-5.2 to score 3 \u2014 rejected: structured-marker convention is form-agnostic. Applying it to Candidate A would push A''s C-5.2 score from 1 to 3 as well, restoring the dominance. The hybrid does not structurally enable structured markers.", "Test H2 (RDF + SQLite) before dismissing D entirely \u2014 rejected by transitivity: H2''s operational scores are necessarily worse than H1''s; H2 cannot score 3 on C-5.2 because RDF does not natively express sentence-level prose markers; therefore H2 cannot satisfy any criterion H1 cannot satisfy.", "Defer dismissal to Session 7 with all four candidates compared \u2014 rejected: D-0112 explicitly specified \"dismissed without further evaluation\" precisely to prevent weighted-total smuggling of strictly-dominated candidates into selection comparisons."]', 'DG-REVIEW because dismissal of a framework-named candidate is a methodological commitment binding Sessions 7-9''s input set. Project-owner review confirms the eliminator test applied correctly. The dismissal does NOT mean hybrid storage architectures are universally inferior — it means: for THIS framework, THESE 30 criteria, with THESE weights, the hybrid form Candidate D evaluated produces no per-criterion score that beats the best-of-the-singletons. If a future framework expansion adds a criterion that only a hybrid satisfies, Candidate D should be re-evaluated under a superseding D-METH amendment.
