', 'Three considerations argue for Rule 3 over Rules 1 or 2:
(1) The B-vs-C gap is 3 weighted points (152 vs 155) — at the noise floor of weighting precision. The framework''s §6.3 noted that doctrinal-binding criteria carry weight beyond their numeric weight; selecting on a 3-point gap when the doctrinal anchors (D-03 solo-author binding) pull in the opposite direction (favoring B''s lower cognitive load over C''s expressive richness) would substitute numeric mechanics for structured reasoning.
(2) The A-vs-(B|C) comparison cannot be made on weighted total alone. A''s adapted ceiling (with 19-29 sessions of investment) is in the same zone as B/C untouched (~150-160). The selection between A and B/C is not "which scores higher" but "which adaptation path is more sustainable for solo-author pre-launch operations over 12+ year horizon."
(3) Cost-to-native varies materially (19-29 vs 27-42 vs 34-50 sessions). Mechanical rules ignore this; Rule 3 incorporates it as a binding consideration.
Rule 3 does not pre-decide; it commits Session 8 to reasoning from the doctrinal anchors and the tradeoff structure rather than from the weighted-total ranking alone. Rule 3 is what the framework intended in §7.2''s prohibition on calibration- sketch-driven selection.
', '2026-05-02 02:35', 'opus_4_7_session_2026-05-02', 'opus/150/synth', 150, 'ACTIVE', 'PENDING', '[]', '["D-0108", "D-0110", "D-0136"]', '["workplan/b1-comparative-scoring.md \u00a73.3", "sessions/session_2026-05-02-b1-s7.md", "data/decisions/decision_register.yaml"]', '["Rule 1 (highest weighted total wins \u2192 C) \u2014 rejected: collapses the selection question into a numeric comparison; ignores doctrinal binding force beyond what weights captured; selects C by a 3-point margin (1.7% of max) which is at the noise floor of weighting precision.", "Rule 2 (highest weighted total subject to doctrinal-anchor constraint \u2192 C, since none of three fails a weight-3 criterion at score < 2) \u2014 rejected: same outcome as Rule 1 because the constraint is non-binding; the doctrinal anchors require structured reasoning, not pass/fail filtering.", "Rule 4 (lowest cost-to-native subject to acceptable score threshold \u2192 A if threshold is 75%, B if threshold is 88%) \u2014 rejected: threshold selection is itself arbitrary without further reasoning; Rule 3 incorporates cost as one consideration among others rather than as a binary filter.", "Defer rule selection to Session 8 alongside the selection itself \u2014 rejected: bundling the rule choice with the selection conflates methodology with outcome; explicit rule commitment in Session 7 makes Session 8''s reasoning auditable against a stated rule."]', 'DG-REVIEW because the selection decision rule constrains Session 8''s reasoning structure. Project-owner review confirms Rule 3 is the appropriate frame for the final selection. If review surfaces concerns, the rule can be amended before Session 8 commits to a candidate.
