Selection rationale walks four steps under D-0137''s Rule 3 (tradeoff-aware selection): (1) Candidate A is eliminated because it scores 2/2/2 on the three weight-3 doctrinal criteria operationalizing mission §Doctrinal commitments 1, 3, 6 — adaptation closes the quantitative gap (19-29 sessions of cell materialization, pre-commit hook, query-layer build) but leaves the corpus structure as markdown+YAML with custom Python query code, accumulating project-specific technical debt over 12+ year horizon; doctrine should be embodied by the form, not adapted to. (2) Candidates B (152/171, 89%) and C (155/171, 91%) both natively satisfy doctrine (3/3/3 on weight-3 criteria); selection is operational not doctrinal. (3) The B-vs-C Cluster IX gap (B 48% vs C 33%) is structural and recurring — CI tractability (B''s SQLite build vs C''s JVM+Jena+SHACL toolchain), solo-author operability (SQL broadly known vs SPARQL+SHACL+ontology specialized), A10/A12/A13 integration (mechanical SQL migration vs ontology-design methodological work). (4) Recurring costs (paid daily over 12+ year horizon) outweigh structural advantages (paid once-per-feature) under D-03''s "solo-only-permanent is a possible end-state" binding; the framework''s weighting captures decision priority but not temporal frequency. The 3-weighted-point gap C-over-B is at the noise floor of weighting precision (1.7% of max) and is correctly broken in B''s favor by the recurring-cost analysis.
Selection: B (Relational / SQLite). Within-B sub-substrate: SQLite per D-0120. Storage form decision is irrevocable in the sense that synthesis structure for 12+ years is what''s being committed; revisable via formal D-METH amendment if the project''s posture changes materially. Migration-out cost is bounded per D-0111''s 5-deliverable standard (PASS).
', 'The full rationale is the proposal-of-record document at architecture/storage-derivation.md (333 lines, 9 sections). Summary by section:
§2 Doctrinal foundations: Three weight-3 doctrinal commitments (DC-1 within-population variability; DC-3 Co-1 evidence co-primacy; DC-6 questions-led teaching) + one operational binding (D-03 pre-launch solo, solo-only-permanent end-state) + one sustainability commitment (S-01 12+ year horizon). The doctrine pulls toward expressive richness; the operational binding pulls toward minimum cognitive surface. The framework''s tiered weighting (3/2/1) is the priority structure that walks this tension.
§3 Candidate inventory: Three single-substrate candidates (A markdown+YAML, B relational/SQLite, C graph/RDF) plus dismissed hybrid (D, dismissed under D-0134). Within-B: SQLite (D-0120 dismissed PostgreSQL, DuckDB). Within-C: RDF/Apache Jena (D-0131 dismissed Blazegraph, Neo4j, Memgraph, TigerGraph).
§4 Evaluation outcome: A 132/171 (77%); B 152/171 (89%); C 155/171 (91%); D dismissed (143/171 informational). Cost-to-near-native: A 19-29 sessions; B 27-42; C 34-50. Critical observations: B has zero unique wins (B never strictly outscores both A and C on any criterion); A has 5 unique wins (all Cluster IX operational, +11 weighted advantage); C has 1 unique win (C-8.1 A9 versioning, +2 weighted advantage). On the three weight-3 doctrinal criteria: A 2/2/2; B 3/3/3; C 3/3/3.
§5 Selection rationale: Four-step Rule 3 walk producing selection of B.
§6 Dismissed alternatives: Each candidate''s dismissal reason; sub-substrate dismissals within Candidates B and C.
§7 Scope limits: Eight explicit non-decisions deferred to Sessions 9, B2-B5, Stage C, or future amendment — schema spec details, tooling design, pilot construction, migration phase ordering, sub-substrate re-evaluation conditions, Candidate C re-evaluation conditions, fate of pre-pivot markdown specs, long-horizon doctrinal revision.
§8 Status and adoption: PROVISIONAL pending project-owner DG-NON adoption. Adoption converts to CANONICAL; denial reverts to status-quo. Triggers CS-MIG cross-stage thread for migration-survival.md reclassification under the SQLite target.
